  than one person — and later, it's the most likely default answer to "tasks I care about."

  [1mThe fork:[0m how a task becomes someone else's business. This sets your permission checks, your API shape, and your
  foreign keys, so it's structural.

  - [1mShared lists/projects[0m — tasks live in a project; you're a member of projects. Membership grants visibility, and
    "care about" defaults to project membership plus assignment. Conventional, and the model most task apps converge on
    because it scales to teams without per-task bookkeeping.
  - [1mPer-task sharing[0m — a task has an owner and an explicit share list, no container. Simpler schema, more granular. But
    it gets tedious fast at any real volume, and there's no natural home for "everything the team is working on."
  - [1mOne global workspace[0m — everyone sees every task; assignment is the only relationship. Simplest possible thing that
    still honors multi-user. Fine for a small trusted group, poor once there's anything private.

  Worth noting for later: whichever you pick becomes the backbone of the notifications subscription model. Projects give
  you a ready-made "watch this project" concept for free; the global workspace gives you nothing to subscribe to except
  individual tasks.

  [1mMy recommendation: shared lists/projects.[0m It's the smallest model that makes both permissions and subscriptions
  meaningful, and it doesn't lock you out of per-task sharing if you want it later.
